  "query_preview": "..\nThrough tonight: Heat and humidity will remain the main concern in \nthe near term. Early morning satellite imagery/RAP analysis showed \nthe circulation around a large 597dam high centered over the central \nPlains. Our CWA will remain on the eastern periphery of this large \nhigh through the forecast period. Satellite imagery also showed a \ndisturbance dropping around the eastern side of the high over the \nTennessee valley. This has resulted in convection nearing northeast \nMississippi already. The bulk of this activity should track just \neast of our CWA but due to an outflow boundary, some of the \nconvection may spread into the eastern portions of our CWA today.\nEarly morning surface analysis had high centered over the northern \nGulf. This surface high will remain in place through the forecast \nperiod. Together high pressure surface and aloft will contribute to \ntemperatures topping out in the mid 90s to near 100F in spots.\n\nA \"Heat Advisory\" remains in effect across the forecast area this \nafternoon as indices range between 106F-110F. The exception to this \nis currently across Bolivar, Sunflower, & Leflore counties where \nafternoon heat index values will climb above 110F. Here, an \"Extreme \nHeat Warning\" is in effect today. /22/\n\nA \"Heat Advisory\" remains in effect across the forecast area this \nafternoon and Monday as indices range between 106F-110F. The \nexception to this is currently across Bolivar, Sunflower, & Leflore \ncounties where afternoon heat index values will climb above 110F. \nHere, an \"Extreme Heat Warning\" is in effect Monday. In addition, on \nthe latest package, confidence in indices climbing above 110F has \nincreased across a larger portion of the CWA for tomorrow. \nTherefore, we've expanded the \"Extreme Heat Warning\" over some \ncentral and western portions of the area tomorrow, with a \"Heat \nAdvisory\" in effect elsewhere.\n\nThursday through Monday...\n\nThe aforementioned high aloft will weaken its presence across region \nas it retreats westward.  This will allow troughing along the east \ncoast to deepen across the Southeastern United States.  As this \nhappens, another surface boundary will attempt to sink south into \nthe CWA. This will increase rain chances across the area, \npotentially bringing at least some temporary relief to the heat. One \nthing of note during this time, while chances for isolated to \nscattered convection will increase, the increased northwest flow \naloft across the region could yield an MCS or two affecting the \nforecast area. This is still many days out and will continue to be \nmonitored in future model data, as we draw near to the weekend.",
